    explanation: "The Wilks score is powerlifting's answer to a hard question: how do you compare the strength of a 60-kilogram lifter with that of a 120-kilogram lifter when the heavier athlete will almost always lift more in absolute terms? This calculator computes it from your bodyweight, the total you lifted, and your sex, returning a single points figure that allows fair comparison across every weight class.\n\nHeavier people can generally lift more, but not in direct proportion to their extra mass, so raw totals favour the biggest athletes. The Wilks formula corrects for this by scaling your lift with a coefficient derived from a fifth-order polynomial fitted to elite performance data, with separate constants for men and women. A lighter lifter earns a higher coefficient, so a modest absolute total from a small athlete can outscore a larger total from a heavy one, reflecting relative strength.\n\nThe result is widely used to decide best-lifter awards at meets, to rank athletes across categories, and to let individuals track their own progress as bodyweight fluctuates. A score around 300 marks a solid intermediate lifter, 400 is advanced, and elite competitors push past 500. Because it isolates strength relative to size, the Wilks score is a fairer benchmark of pound-for-pound ability than any raw total, and this calculator makes it instantly available for training logs and competition planning.",
